course: geography
lesson: 50
title: Minerals and crystals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Rocks are made of minerals, the way a fruit cake is made of flour, sugar and raisins: a mineral is a natural solid with a fixed chemical makeup and its atoms arranged in a regular pattern.
explain: When atoms line up in a repeating pattern, the solid grows flat faces and sharp edges: that is a crystal, and the pattern decides its shape, like cubes of salt or six-sided columns of quartz.
explain: Sprinkle salt into warm water, leave the saucer on a sunny windowsill for a week, and tiny cubes appear as the water evaporates; crystals grow by adding atoms layer by layer.
explain: Geologists tell minerals apart like detectives: how hard they are, their colour, their shine, the colour of their powder (streak) and how they break.
explain: That is how they catch fool's gold, pyrite, which glitters like gold but is harder, much lighter and leaves a black streak.
fact: A mineral is a natural solid with a fixed chemical makeup and atoms arranged in a regular pattern.
fact: A crystal is a solid whose atoms are lined up in a repeating pattern, giving flat faces and sharp edges; in glass the atoms are jumbled with no pattern, so glass is not a crystal.
fact: Scientists know about 6,000 minerals, but only a few dozen are common.
fact: Quartz is one of the most common minerals, made of silicon and oxygen, and most sand is quartz.
fact: Feldspar is the most common mineral group in the Earth's crust.
fact: The Mohs scale ranks minerals from 1 (talc, the softest) to 10 (diamond, the hardest) by which can scratch which.
fact: Geologists identify minerals by hardness, colour, lustre (shine), streak and how they break.
fact: Streak is the colour of a mineral's powder, seen by rubbing it on rough white tile, and it is often more reliable than the colour of the lump.
fact: Pyrite is called fool's gold because it looks like gold and often grows in shiny cubes, but it is harder, lighter and leaves a black streak, while gold leaves a gold-yellow streak.
fact: Rock salt (halite) is a mineral that formed when ancient seas dried up.
fact: Crystals grow when a liquid cools or a solution evaporates, adding atoms layer by layer.
fact: The Cave of Crystals in Naica, Mexico, holds gypsum crystals up to about 12 metres long, which grew very slowly for hundreds of thousands of years in warm mineral water.
exercise: What is a mineral? | A natural solid with a fixed chemical makeup and a regular atom pattern | Any piece of rock you find on the ground, whatever it is made of | Anything made by a factory from metal | A liquid with a fixed makeup found deep underground in caves
exercise: What gives a crystal its flat faces and sharp edges? | Its atoms lined up in a repeating pattern | Someone cutting and polishing it by hand | Being frozen in ice | Air bubbles trapped inside it pushing out
exercise: About how many minerals do scientists know? | About 6,000, but only a few dozen are common | Exactly 10, one for each number on the Mohs scale | About 60 million | About 6, one per continent
exercise: What is quartz made of? | Silicon and oxygen | Iron and sulfur only | Sodium and chlorine | Carbon and nothing else
exercise: What is most sand made of? | Quartz | Gold dust | Sea salt | Diamond
exercise: Which is the most common mineral group in the Earth's crust? | Feldspar | Quartz | Pyrite | Diamond
exercise: What does the Mohs scale measure? | Hardness, by which mineral can scratch which | Weight, by putting minerals on kitchen scales | Age, in millions of years | Shine, from dull to bright
exercise: Which minerals sit at the two ends of the Mohs scale? | Talc at 1 and diamond at 10 | Diamond at 1 and talc at 10 | Quartz at 1 and gold at 10 | Salt at 1 and iron at 10
exercise: Which clues do geologists use to identify minerals? | Hardness, colour, lustre, streak and how they break | Only their colour, as each mineral has just one | Only their smell | Only where they were found and how deep they lay
exercise: What is a mineral's streak? | The colour of its powder rubbed on rough white tile | A crack running right through the middle of the crystal | The shine on its surface | A stripe of colour across the lump
exercise: Why is pyrite called fool's gold? | It looks like gold but is not | It is worth much more than gold | Fools once used it as their money | It was found by a man called Fool
exercise: How can you tell pyrite from gold? | Pyrite is harder, lighter and leaves a black streak | Pyrite is softer and heavier than gold, and leaves no streak | Pyrite leaves a gold streak and gold a black one | You cannot tell them apart at all
exercise: How did rock salt (halite) form? | Ancient seas dried up and left the salt | Salt rained down from the sky in storms | Lava cooled into salt | Plants rotted and turned into salt
exercise: How do crystals grow? | Atoms add on layer by layer as a liquid cools or a solution evaporates | They are carved out of rock by running water | They grow from tiny seeds in the ground, just like plants grow from theirs | They form in one second in lightning
exercise: What is in the Cave of Crystals in Naica, Mexico? | Gypsum crystals up to about 12 metres long | Diamonds as big as cars | A river of liquid quartz | Golden crystals made of pyrite, piled up to the roof
exercise: Which mineral is hardest of all on the Mohs scale? | Diamond | Clear quartz | Soft talc | Feldspar
test: "found gold in the stream!!! shiny cubes, gonna be rich" What should you check first? | Its streak and hardness: shiny cubes are probably pyrite | Nothing, all shiny yellow rock is gold, so start spending | Whether it floats, gold floats | Whether it smells of eggs, gold does
test: Your friend says: "Every rock is a mineral." Put her right. | Rocks are usually mixtures of minerals, like granite of quartz, feldspar and mica | She's right, rock and mineral mean the same | Minerals are made of rocks, not the other way round, so every mineral is a rock | Minerals are only found in jewellery
test: A mystery mineral scratches glass but is scratched by quartz. What can you say about it? | It is softer than quartz but harder than glass | It must be diamond, the hardest of all | It must be talc | It is harder than quartz and softer than glass
test: What would happen if you let a saucer of salty water evaporate on a windowsill? | Small cube-shaped salt crystals would grow | The salt would slowly turn into quartz | Nothing, salt disappears with the water | The water would turn into tiny ice crystals
test: "two yellow shiny rocks same size, one's way heavier. which is gold?" | The heavier one; gold is much heavier than pyrite | The lighter one; gold is a very light metal, like foil | They weigh the same, you can't tell | The one that sparkles more in the sun
test: A cartoon miner scratches a diamond with his fingernail. What's wrong? | Diamond is the hardest mineral; a fingernail can't scratch it | Nothing, diamond is soft like talc | Diamond can only ever be scratched by gold, not by nails | Fingernails are harder than any mineral
test: A crystal and a piece of glass both look clear. What is the real difference inside? | The crystal's atoms are in a repeating pattern; the glass's are jumbled | The glass has a pattern; the crystal is jumbled | There is no difference at all | The crystal is liquid inside
test: How could deep layers of rock salt end up under dry land in England? | An ancient sea there dried up and left its salt behind | Salt lorries buried it there long ago to store it for winter | Rain carried salt down from the clouds | It froze out of underground ice
test: Why did the Naica crystals grow so huge? | They grew very slowly for a very long time in warm mineral water | Someone glued lots of small crystals together | They were made in a factory and hidden there | They all grew in a single night after a big earthquake shook the cave
test: Leo says quartz is a rare gem you only find in shops. What's true? | Quartz is one of the most common minerals, and most sand is quartz | He's right, it's rarer than diamond | Quartz doesn't occur naturally at all, it is only made in factories | Quartz is only found on the Moon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a mineral? | A naturally occurring solid of fixed chemical composition with an ordered atomic structure | Any solid substance extracted from the ground irrespective of composition | A manufactured solid of variable composition | A naturally occurring liquid of fixed chemical composition
exam: Identify the property ranked by the Mohs scale. | Hardness, as determined by the capacity of one mineral to scratch another | Density, as determined by weighing in water | Lustre, as determined by the reflection of light from the surface of the specimen | Age, as determined by radioactive decay
exam: It is given that a yellow metallic specimen leaves a black streak upon an unglazed tile. Ascertain its most probable identity. | Pyrite | Pure gold | Halite salt | White talc
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the formation of halite deposits. | They were formed by the evaporation of ancient seas | They were formed by the cooling and crystallisation of basaltic lava | They were formed by the compaction of shells | They were formed by the metamorphism of slate
